<BaseLayout title="Fourier Analysis" description="Fourier series, transforms, convolution, sampling, reciprocal space, and FFTs for electronic structure." current="theory">
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href={href('/theory/')}>Theory</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Fourier Analysis</span>
  </nav>

  <h1>Fourier Analysis</h1>
  <p class="lede">Fourier analysis changes the representation of a function from position or time to spatial frequency, momentum, or reciprocal-space components. In electronic structure it connects periodicity, plane waves, convolution, diffraction, grids, and fast transforms.</p>
  <p>The transform does not create new physics. It reorganizes the same object in a representation where translation, differentiation, or convolution may be simpler.</p>

  <h2>Periodic functions become discrete reciprocal components</h2>
  <p>For a function periodic on a crystal lattice, a Fourier series has the form</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="f of r equals sum over reciprocal lattice vectors of f G times exponential i G dot r">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo>
          <munderover><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo></mo></munderover>
          <msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi></msub>
          <msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=\sum_\mathbf&#123;G&#125; f_\mathbf&#123;G&#125;e^&#123;i\mathbf&#123;G&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;r&#125;&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The allowed vectors <math class="math-inline" aria-label="G"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;G&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> belong to the reciprocal lattice. A smooth periodic field may require relatively few large-scale components; rapidly varying or singular structure produces substantial high-frequency content.</p>

  <h2>The continuous transform uses a convention that must be stated</h2>
  <p>One common three-dimensional convention is</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="f tilde of k equals integral f of r exponential minus i k dot r d cubed r">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mover><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo>
          <mo>∫</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo>
          <msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi></mrow></msup>
          <msup><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>r</mi><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde f(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)=\int f(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)e^&#123;-i\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;r&#125;&#125;d^3r.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Factors of <math class="math-inline" aria-label="two pi"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, signs, and normalization may be distributed differently between forward and inverse transforms. Mixing conventions silently changes coefficients, reciprocal vectors, and dimensional units.</p>

  <h2>Differentiation becomes multiplication</h2>
  <p>For sufficiently regular functions and compatible boundary conditions, spatial differentiation maps to multiplication by the wavevector:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Fourier transform of gradient f equals i k times f tilde">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi><mo>[</mo><mo>∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mover><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal&#123;F&#125;[\nabla f]=i\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\tilde f.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>This is why plane waves diagonalize the kinetic-energy operator and why Poisson equations can be simple in reciprocal space. The simplification can fail at singular components or at wavevectors where the inverse operator is undefined, such as the zero-frequency component of periodic electrostatics.</p>

  <h2>Convolution becomes a product</h2>
  <p>With a consistent convention,</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Fourier transform of convolution f star g equals f tilde times g tilde">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi mathvariant="script">F</mi><mo>[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mover><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal&#123;F&#125;[f*g]=\tilde f\,\tilde g.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Hartree potentials, filtering, pseudopotential operations, structure factors, and response kernels repeatedly use this conversion. Multiplication in one representation corresponds to convolution in the other; it is not generally valid to treat both as pointwise products.</p>

  <h2>Dirac distributions and Poisson summation encode periodic repetition</h2>
  <p>An ideal lattice can be represented by a sum of translated delta distributions. Its Fourier transform is another lattice in reciprocal space. This is the mathematical reason that perfect translational order produces discrete diffraction conditions.</p>
  <p>Delta functions are distributions rather than ordinary finite-valued functions. Formal manipulations remain useful, but normalization, integration domains, and dimensional factors must be controlled.</p>

  <h2>The DFT and FFT are finite numerical objects</h2>
  <p>The discrete Fourier transform maps a finite sampled sequence to a finite set of frequency coefficients:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="F m equals sum n equals zero to N minus one f n exponential minus two pi i m n over N">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo>
          <munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover>
          <msub><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msub>
          <msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_m=\sum_&#123;n=0&#125;^&#123;N-1&#125;f_n e^&#123;-2\pi i mn/N&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The FFT is an algorithm for computing this transform efficiently. It does not decide whether the grid is fine enough, whether aliasing is controlled, or whether a periodic cell represents the intended physical system.</p>

  <h2>Sampling creates a reciprocal periodicity</h2>
  <p>Sampling in real space repeats spectral content in reciprocal space. Frequencies above the Nyquist limit can fold into lower frequencies and become aliasing error. Conversely, truncating reciprocal components limits real-space resolution and can produce ringing near sharp features.</p>
  <p>Plane-wave cutoffs, FFT grids, augmentation grids, density cutoffs, and interpolation grids are related but not interchangeable parameters. Their relation depends on the representation and implementation.</p>

  <h2>Reciprocal lattice and Brillouin-zone variables are not the same index</h2>
  <p>A reciprocal-lattice vector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="G"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;G&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> labels Fourier components of a lattice-periodic function. A crystal momentum <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> labels a Bloch sector and is defined modulo reciprocal-lattice vectors. Confusing the two obscures the distinction between a plane-wave basis inside one Bloch state and sampling different Bloch states across the Brillouin zone.</p>

  <h2>Study routes</h2>
  <p><a href="https://see.stanford.edu/Course/EE261">Stanford Engineering Everywhere EE261, <cite>The Fourier Transform and Its Applications</cite></a> is the strongest open course route in the reviewed report. It covers Fourier series and transforms, distributions, convolution, sampling, the DFT/FFT, multidimensional transforms, and crystallographic applications.</p>
  <p><a href="https://www.iucr.org/education/pamphlets/4">IUCr Teaching Pamphlet 4, <cite>The Reciprocal Lattice</cite></a> is a concise bridge from direct and reciprocal lattices to diffraction. It assumes vector fluency and is not a complete Fourier-analysis course.</p>
  <p><a href="https://www.cambridge.org/highereducation/books/electronic-structure/0F1D0525EC0BF4084A0F2D00637E5D0F">Richard M. Martin, <cite>Electronic Structure</cite>, 2nd ed.</a> supplies the electronic-structure context for plane waves, periodicity, Coulomb terms, grids, and reciprocal-space methods. It is paid or institutionally accessed.</p>
  <p><a href="https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~jos/mdft/">Julius O. Smith III, <cite>Mathematics of the Discrete Fourier Transform</cite></a> is an author-hosted online text from CCRMA that develops the DFT from complex numbers, vectors, projections, roots of unity, convolution, sampling, and aliasing before discussing FFT algorithms. It is valuable when the finite DFT matrix must be distinguished from the continuous transform and from the FFT implementation. Its examples are drawn largely from audio and signal processing, so reciprocal lattices and Brillouin-zone integration still need the subject-specific routes on this page.</p>

  <h2>Boundary of this page</h2>
  <p>This page does not treat wavelets, harmonic analysis on general groups, FFT implementation optimization, or a full plane-wave method. It establishes the transform, convolution, sampling, reciprocal-space, and aliasing concepts that those later topics require.</p>

  <aside class="review-note">
    <p><strong>Review note.</strong> Stanford EE261, the IUCr reciprocal-lattice pamphlet, and the Cambridge Martin destination were reviewed on 3 August 2026. Resource availability does not by itself validate a numerical cutoff or grid for a particular observable.</p>
  </aside>
